[38;5;231m⏺[39m This is a bounded change, so I'll give you a short design here in chat and won't write a spec file. You can tell me if
  you'd rather go the heavier route.

  I've opened a browser tab with three sketches of where the filter controls could go:
  [94m]8;id=6mgjw6;http://localhost:52627/?key=2eaf7bd98f0b81447c43f6e3c435415a7bcd00ceb9cd3edb9026c13a5baf5d54\http://localhost:52627/?key=2eaf7bd98f0b81447c43f6e3c435415a7bcd00ceb9cd3edb9026c13a5baf5d54[39m]8;;\

  - [1mA. Filter bar above the table.[0m An event-type dropdown, a search box, and a "Show only failed sign-ins" checkbox are
    always visible. A line under them says how many events are showing.
  - [1mB. A "Filter" button.[0m It opens a panel with the controls, and each active filter shows as a tag you can remove. This
    takes less space, but finding failed sign-ins takes two clicks.
  - [1mC. Quick group buttons.[0m One click picks a fixed group: All, Sign-ins, Failed sign-ins, Account changes or API
    tokens. There's no search and no way to pick a single event type.

  I'd go with A. Security wants failed sign-ins easy to spot, and the only support tickets came from people looking for
  a failed sign-in they didn't recognize. A puts that one click away with nothing to open first.

  Look at the sketches and tell me here which one fits. You can also click one in the browser and I'll pick up the
  selection. After that I'll ask, one at a time, which filters to include and whether to fix the existing problem where
  the table is empty with JavaScript off.

  The sketches are saved under [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in the repo. That folder isn't in [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m yet (the repo doesn't have
  one), so add it before you commit, or I can add it for you.

